    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Hello and welcome to the Shift Makers Podcast where we share the collective wisdom some of our greatest minds have to offer. I'm your host Marianne Schnall, a writer and journalist. Over the years, I've had the incredible honor of interviewing a variety of remarkable change makers and it is my pleasure to share some of these recordings with you for this podcast. Welcome to Shift Makers. In honor of former President Jimmy Carter's 98th birthday, I wanted to reshare my interview with President Carter, our longest living president, whose life, leadership, and lifetime of service is certainly worth celebrating. This interview, while recorded eight years ago in 2014, covers themes that are as relevant today as they were back then. As I am developing the third season of shift makers, I wanted to update this feed with highlights from seasons one and two to elevate and expand on my former guests' timeless wisdom. The following introduction is from my 2020 release of this podcast recording. Welcome to the podcast. This is Mary Ann Schnall. I was personally so struck and inspired and moved by this conversation. And I think it's also to appreciate it in the context of the history of who this man is, you know, since leaving the White House in 1981, President Carter, who was the 39th President of the United States, has been one of our most active and involved presidents. whether it's been through his work with founding the Carter Center, which works to advance peace and health worldwide, or the fact that he has authored 28 books and also received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2002 for his humanitarian work, he has been dedicated to creating change for his entire life and career. And to me, that makes it almost particularly remarkable that in his later years, he decided that of all of the issues that are obviously so pressing and of concern that he was going to dedicate himself to focus his attention on what he calls the most serious and unaddressed worldwide challenge of our time, which he believes is the discrimination and abuse of women and girls worldwide. When I spoke to him for this interview, it was around a book that he had written on this topic called A Call to Action, Women, Religion, Violence and Power. And this book was informed by his travels to over 145 countries and sort of witnessing firsthand with his own eyes, a system of discrimination in which he believes that women are deprived of all of their basic rights, deprived of education, healthcare, equal opportunity, and farce to suffer in servitude or experienced child marriage or trapped in cycles of poverty, war and violence for themselves and their families, the infanticide of millions of newborn girls and selective abortion of female fetuses, female genital relation, the global pandemic of rape, including rape being used as a weapon of war and the worldwide trafficking and women and young girls, and also look at what he sees as the root causes, so for example, women being considered and treated as second-class citizens and cultures throughout the world, which also includes the incorrect interpretation of religious texts, which often preach that men are superior to women in the eyes of God, which is then often used to justify their subjugation and abuse, and then just the general excessive resort to violence of all kinds, which sort of permeates our culture. And now let's join my conversation with President Jimmy Carter. So",
